In detectMergeConflicts, the clean-merge path is: `cmd('git merge origin/... --no-commit --no-ff')` succeeds → log line. For my up-to-date detection, I'll add after the clean merge:

```js
// Log hygiene (gh-802 AC3a): distinguish a merge that actually brought base
// changes (staged) from an already-up-to-date branch (nothing staged) — the
// old log claimed "base branch changes staged" even when the merge was a no-op.
var staged = true;
try {
    cmd('git diff --cached --quiet HEAD');
    staged = false; // exit 0 — nothing staged: branch was already up to date
} catch (eQuiet) {
    staged = true;  // exit 1 — the merge staged base changes
}
console.log(staged
    ? 'No merge conflicts — base branch changes staged'
    : 'No merge conflicts — branch already up to date with origin/' + baseBranch);
```

Mock: cli_execute_command returns output; when does the real cli_execute_command THROW? On non-zero exit. In tests, a throwing mock simulates exit 1. For the quiet diff: exit 0 → return ''; exit 1 → throw.

Hmm wait — one consideration: `git diff --cached --quiet HEAD` exit codes: 0 = no differences, 1 = differences. What if git errors (exit >1, e.g. not a repo)? Then treat as staged (conservative — keeps the old wording). The try/catch approach handles that: any throw → staged=true → old wording. Good conservative behavior.

